ki'e Pierre
 
I had thought of using {ke'a} .iku'ibabo I realized that {ke'a} works 
specifically in reletive clauses to refer to the sumti being modified - and 
thus it would not work in this case.  So I looked up {ce'u} in the 
Reference Guide and sure enough, that would do it!  Thanks again!
 
mi'e .neit. mu'o

On Wednesday, April 23, 2014 12:34:24 PM UTC-4, Pierre Abbat wrote:

> On Tuesday, April 22, 2014 09:12:38 neit wrote: 
